/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   It is there because a constant inside a leading comment can swallow the
   rest of the file in the engine - see RMSTEST_56 and the directory README.
   Nothing below the script can do any harm. */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

  create_land
  {
    terrain_type GRASS
    base_size 4
    number_of_tiles 300
    zone 2
    land_position 20 20
  }
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type DIRT
    base_size 4
    number_of_tiles 300
    zone 3
    land_position 80 50
  }
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type GRASS2
    base_size 4
    number_of_tiles 300
    zone 4
    land_position 20 80
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>


<CONNECTION_GENERATION>

  create_connect_all_lands
  {
    replace_terrain GRASS ROAD
    replace_terrain GRASS2 ROAD
    terrain_cost DIRT 0
  }

/*
================================================================================
RMSTEST_75a - does a cost-0 terrain under a land's origin block EVERY
connection touching that land, not just the one the guide's own worked
example happens to describe?
Feeds: docs/known-issues.md BUG-029, preview-design.md Sec.6.5
(src/preview/generator/connections.ts:520, "Departing and arriving are the
same test, the origin tile's own passability")
Paired with RMSTEST_75b, its control.
================================================================================

THE QUESTION
guide:1937 - "A cost of 0 will prevent the connection generation from
running through that terrain. If a land origin is on such a terrain, or
such a terrain MUST be crossed, then the connections to that land will not
be generated at all." The shipped model treats this as symmetric - a
land's own origin-tile passability governs both departing FROM it and
arriving AT it, because both are now modelled as the same multi-source
Dijkstra closing a goal tile (Sec.6.5). That symmetry has never been run
against the real engine; the withdrawn per-tile model had it asymmetric
(depart from anywhere the land's tiles touched, arrive only through the
land's own passable tiles), so an implementation bug of exactly that shape
is not a remote worry.

THREE LANDS, ONE COMMAND. A and C are ordinary GRASS/GRASS2 lands, far
apart, with nothing between them. B sits between neither of them
geometrically (it is off to the side at land_position 80 50, while A and C
both sit at x=20) and its entire body is DIRT - the only DIRT on the map.
create_connect_all_lands connects ALL THREE PAIRS (A-B, B-C, A-C).
terrain_cost DIRT 0 makes B's whole body - origin included, since a land's
origin is one of the tiles it owns - impassable.

PREDICTIONS
  BLOCKED, BOTH DIRECTIONS (guide's claim holds) -> exactly one road forms,
    A-C. No ROAD tile ever comes within a tile or two of B's DIRT patch,
    from either side of it.
  BLOCKED ONE WAY ONLY (the withdrawn model's asymmetry survived the
    rewrite as a latent bug) -> a road reaches B from one of A or C but not
    the other - e.g. A's search (seeded at A, B as one of its goals) closes
    B's origin despite the 0 cost, while C's own search never does, or vice
    versa. Distinguishable from "blocked both ways" only by checking BOTH
    of B's potential neighbours, not just one - which is the whole reason
    this script uses three lands and not two.
  NOT BLOCKED AT ALL -> three separate roads, one for every pair, and DIRT
    sits directly on at least one of them.

B is placed off the A-C line on purpose, so a road that only ever runs
between A and C - and never approaches B - cannot be mistaken for one that
grazes B by geometric accident.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), ANY PLAYER COUNT. THREE RUNS.
Three rather than two: A* has no reason to be non-deterministic here (no
randomness in the search itself), but land growth is, and RMSTEST_51's
"regenerate between saves" lesson means the read should survive more than
one export before it is trusted.

READ WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --bbox DIRT
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches ROAD
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches GRASS
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches GRASS2
  Compare every ROAD patch's bounding box against DIRT's. A gap of at least
  a couple of tiles on every side, on every run, is "blocked". Any ROAD
  patch whose bbox touches or overlaps DIRT's is "not blocked" from that
  side - note WHICH side (nearer A or nearer C) so the direction question
  is actually answered rather than just the total-block one.
================================================================================
*/
